WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.113
Welcome to episode two.

2
00:00:03.113 --> 00:00:08.640
Today we slow down and really learn chat:
you’ll ask the same question twice and see

3
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.601
why one answer is worth
more, check a source,

4
00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:14.562
put two models head to
head — and build this:

5
00:00:14.562 --> 00:00:18.444
a one-page brief, written onto
the canvas next to the chat.

6
00:00:18.444 --> 00:00:20.155
That’s where we’ll end up.

7
00:00:20.155 --> 00:00:23.510
We’ll go step by step,
starting from an empty chat.

8
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.529
Here’s where every chat starts.

9
00:00:31.529 --> 00:00:36.541
Before we type anything, look at the
bottom bar — it holds three choices.

10
00:00:36.541 --> 00:00:41.141
Which agent answers, which model
runs, and what context you attach.

11
00:00:41.141 --> 00:00:44.574
Those three decide what
kind of answer comes back.

12
00:00:44.574 --> 00:00:46.360
So let’s put them to work.

13
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.685
First, an experiment.

14
00:00:51.685 --> 00:00:57.132
We ask about our own customers —
without giving the model anything to read.

15
00:00:57.132 --> 00:01:00.255
We send it… and watch
the answer stream in.

16
00:01:00.255 --> 00:01:01.998
Sounds good, doesn’t it?

17
00:01:01.998 --> 00:01:04.830
Confident, well structured, reasonable.

18
00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.491
Now read it with me —
because this is the trap.

19
00:01:10.491 --> 00:01:13.854
Not one number in there
comes from your workspace.

20
00:01:13.854 --> 00:01:19.437
The model has never seen your customers,
so it fills the gap with patterns from its

21
00:01:19.437 --> 00:01:20.379
training data.

22
00:01:20.379 --> 00:01:22.800
It sounds right — and it’s guessing.

23
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:26.565
Let’s fix that.

24
00:01:26.565 --> 00:01:32.314
Same topic, fresh chat — but this time we
type an at-sign first and attach the Q2

25
00:01:32.314 --> 00:01:33.379
support review.

26
00:01:33.379 --> 00:01:37.849
The document now rides along
with the question… and we send it.

27
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.026
Look at the difference.

28
00:01:44.026 --> 00:01:48.736
Webhook questions doubled after the
April release — that’s your number,

29
00:01:48.736 --> 00:01:49.864
from your review.

30
00:01:49.864 --> 00:01:53.844
The answer names its sources
and ends with one concrete fix.

31
00:01:53.844 --> 00:01:55.237
Same model as before.

32
00:01:55.237 --> 00:01:58.421
The only thing we
changed is what it could read.

33
00:02:02.321 --> 00:02:06.490
Here’s what a grounded answer
unlocks: you can question it.

34
00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:10.306
In the same thread, we ask
— which document says that?

35
00:02:10.306 --> 00:02:14.404
And the reply names the exact
file, the Q2 support review.

36
00:02:14.404 --> 00:02:18.503
When a claim looks off later,
this is the question to ask.

37
00:02:21.103 --> 00:02:23.238
One more habit while we’re here.

38
00:02:23.238 --> 00:02:25.774
This answer held up — so let’s say so.

39
00:02:25.774 --> 00:02:27.909
We click the thumbs-up under it.

40
00:02:27.909 --> 00:02:31.713
That rating lands in your
workspace’s feedback analytics,

41
00:02:31.713 --> 00:02:33.582
and episode nine reads them.

42
00:02:33.582 --> 00:02:37.653
It’s one click — and your team
learns which answers to trust.

43
00:02:41.553 --> 00:02:43.417
Second decision: the model.

44
00:02:43.417 --> 00:02:44.936
Let’s open the picker.

45
00:02:44.936 --> 00:02:49.424
Auto is the default — it routes
each request to a sensible model,

46
00:02:49.424 --> 00:02:52.807
and for everyday
questions that’s the right call.

47
00:02:52.807 --> 00:02:55.983
Pin a specific model
when the task is unusual.

48
00:02:55.983 --> 00:02:57.640
But how do you pick one?

49
00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:58.953
Let’s get evidence.

50
00:03:01.453 --> 00:03:03.911
For evidence, chat has Arena mode.

51
00:03:03.911 --> 00:03:08.249
We start a fresh chat, open
the plus menu… and switch it on.

52
00:03:08.249 --> 00:03:11.719
One prompt now goes to
two models, side by side.

53
00:03:11.719 --> 00:03:16.853
We ask for a launch checklist — and
both columns answer the same brief.

54
00:03:25.453 --> 00:03:27.421
Take your time and compare.

55
00:03:27.421 --> 00:03:31.066
The left one is short and
tidy — five steps, done.

56
00:03:31.066 --> 00:03:36.898
The right one groups the work by phase
and flags two risks the left one skipped:

57
00:03:36.898 --> 00:03:42.147
forty unmapped blog URLs, and no
agreed bar for the accessibility sweep.

58
00:03:42.147 --> 00:03:44.261
For a quick list, A would do.

59
00:03:44.261 --> 00:03:47.031
We’re running a launch — so we vote B.

60
00:03:47.031 --> 00:03:51.043
The verdict feeds the same
analytics as your thumbs-up.

61
00:03:55.753 --> 00:03:57.675
New task: a big deliverable.

62
00:03:57.675 --> 00:04:03.235
We ask for a one-page brief for the
leadership team — the kind of thing you never

63
00:04:03.235 --> 00:04:05.157
want as a wall of chat text.

64
00:04:05.157 --> 00:04:10.648
Send… and watch the right side: the
canvas opens, and the brief lands as a file.

65
00:04:10.648 --> 00:04:13.943
We switch to Preview —
and it reads like a page.

66
00:04:19.453 --> 00:04:22.333
And a canvas file is a working document.

67
00:04:22.333 --> 00:04:24.349
Leadership wants it shorter?

68
00:04:24.349 --> 00:04:28.021
We say so, in plain words:
cut it to three bullets.

69
00:04:28.021 --> 00:04:32.628
The agent rewrites the file in
place — same page, three bullets.

70
00:04:32.628 --> 00:04:37.523
Nothing to copy anywhere; you
refine the document right where it is.

71
00:04:41.523 --> 00:04:43.958
One more to point out: deep research.

72
00:04:43.958 --> 00:04:47.380
Open the plus menu, and
there it is — Deep research.

73
00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:51.197
Turn it on and the chat hands
off to the Researcher agent:

74
00:04:51.197 --> 00:04:54.224
it plans its own
searches, reads the open web,

75
00:04:54.224 --> 00:04:56.857
and returns a report with named sources.

76
00:04:56.857 --> 00:05:01.990
It needs a search connector, so we’ll
give it a full episode of its own later.

77
00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:05.413
Today’s rule — check the
source — applies there too.

78
00:05:08.013 --> 00:05:09.854
And that’s chat, used well.

79
00:05:09.854 --> 00:05:13.400
You asked the same
question ungrounded and grounded,

80
00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.787
checked a source, rated an answer,
judged an Arena — and turned feedback into a

81
00:05:18.787 --> 00:05:22.606
brief on the canvas, then
cut it down with one sentence.

82
00:05:22.606 --> 00:05:27.583
The chat guide in the docs covers all
of it, keyboard shortcuts included.

83
00:05:29.583 --> 00:05:34.707
Next time: Knowledge — where the
documents behind your grounded answers live.

84
00:05:34.707 --> 00:05:38.367
You’ll index one yourself
and watch the answers change.

85
00:05:38.367 --> 00:05:40.031
See you in episode three.

